Now time.js. Careful with Intl.

localDate(utcIso, tz) → 'YYYY-MM-DD'
localDateTime(utcIso, tz) → 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm'

Use formatToParts with en-US? Let's use en-CA for date and manually build.

Implementation:

```js
const cache = new Map();
function formatter(tz) {
  let f = cache.get(tz);
  if (!f) {
    f = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', {
      timeZone: tz, year:'numeric', month:'2-digit', day:'2-digit',
      hour:'2-digit', minute:'2-digit', hour12:false,
    });
    cache.set(tz, f);
  }
  return f;
}
```
en-GB gives dd/mm/yyyy but formatToParts gives components so locale doesn't matter. Use 'en-US'.

hour12:false with en-US may give hour '24' for midnight in some ICU versions. Safer: hourCycle: 'h23'. Node 22 supports hourCycle.

```js
const parts = formatter(tz).formatToParts(new Date(iso));
const map = {};
for (const p of parts) if (p.type !== 'literal') map[p.type] = p.value;
```
Then date = `${map.year}-${map.month}-${map.day}`, time = `${map.hour}:${map.minute}`.

Edge: hour could be '24' → normalize `hour === '24' ? '00'`. Add safety.

Also need utcNowIso() → new Date().toISOString().replace('.000','')? Keep full ISO. Format stored: '2026-01-10T13:30:00.000Z'. For seeded fixed values I'll use exactly '2026-01-10T13:30:00Z'. new Date() parses both. Fine, but for consistency in display only.

For comparisons/sorting we sort by issued_local_date (text) — fine.

Write time.js.

